# A fold that allocates a temporary each time round and threads a map. The
# temporary is never stored: it is bound, its length is read, and it is
# dropped. Threading an int instead, the same loop reclaims every one of them.
#
# Threading a map it now reclaims too: beat_iters below is the bracket being
# emitted, where it read zero for as long as a map's pairs lived in the arena.
#
# The other numbers do not fall here, and that is a property of the size
# rather than of the fix. Twenty thousand iterations of a one-key map fit in a
# single arena block whichever way the storage goes, and one block is the
# floor — so this fixture cannot show the reclaim even now that it happens.
# tests/map_accumulator_peak.rs runs the same program at ten times the count,
# where the gap is nine blocks against one, and asserts the ratio rather than
# a constant.
fn go 0 m
  print (length (entries m))

fn go n m
  junk = "k{n % 17}"
  go (n - 1) (put m "k1" (m["k1"]! + length junk))

pub play = go 20000 { "k1":0 }
